            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

AIR SEALING



Overall, your house is moderately leaky from an air leakage perspective, and I have identified a few locations that can be addressed:



** The largest source of air leakage I could find is the open combustion air pipe (4" in diameter) located near the boiler in your mechanical room on the 1st floor - this pipe is likely a significant cause of this room being cold, as it is constantly dumping cold outdoor air into the room, but it is required by code for the type of boiler in your home.



** Another large air leakage source identified is around window frames and through sliders that weren't latching well. This could be addressed with window upgrades (See Windows upgrade section).



** The bathroom exhaust fan was a bit leaky when the home was depressurized. Bathroom fans can be replaced with energy star rated fans with butterfly style dampers, that seal better than traditional exhaust fan dampers.



** Around the outside edge of the exterior doors. Door replacements could be considered, or weather stripping could be replaced.



** Around the wood stove chimney flue. This could be sealed with high temperature caulking. 



** Another, significant source of air leakage is your floors, which is likely the cause of the cold floors you described. Crawlspace access was limited due to the amount of water underneath, however, some areas to look at in more detail were noted. The floor is well insulated with 3 inches of XPS insulation on the underside and seams are taped, which is excellent attention to detail, however where the XPS butts up against the wooden beams of the home there are gaps (see attached pictures). These gaps could be filled with spray foam, then taped. This observation also made me wonder about how the XPS insulation butts up against the crawlspace walls, and how tight that seal was. It was impossible to investigate without destroying insulation. These two things likely cause your cold floors, and a contractor could be hired to address them, provided you are specific in what you want completed.



Savings: If you address all the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a significant 30% reduction in air leakage heat loss. For modelling purposes I estimated only a 10% reduction in air leakage, which is estimated to save you $270 per year and decrease your annual energy usage by 5.8 GJ. Although the savings aren't huge, the increase in comfort downstairs will be noticeable, for relatively low effort/ expense.



There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1000 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

MAIN WALLS



The main exterior walls of your home, for the purposes of our modelling, appear to be 2x6 construction with batt insulation (R20). Your home has newer, hard board, siding, so this recommendation is only to provide you with an idea of what your savings would be if your walls had a higher insulation value, and is not meant to be something that is highly recommended. Newer homes tend to be constructed with R30- R35 walls, so I have modelled adding 3 additional inches (R15) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation to every exterior surface.



Savings: By adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls, you would save approximately $1000 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 22 GJ, and qualify for up to a $2,500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ÂinsulationÂ rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for adding insulation to exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.

</mainWalls>
                <windows>

WINDOWS



There are 17 total windows between the two floors, and they are a mixture of styles. There are some triple paned windows, many double paned slider windowes and some wood framed windows in the bay window. In general the windows were found to be in good shape, and replacement should be considered only on an as needed basis, considering that windows on an individual level are expensive to replace, and have a small overall impact to your energy costs.



 I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all the windows with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing all existing windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 580 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 12 GJ. 



There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, however there are rebates available from the Greener Homes Grant - Government of Canada. Please see their website for more details.



GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $125 or $250 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.</windows>
                <doors>

DOOR UPGRADE



The doors in your home are all solid wood doors considered to be in good condition, but solid wood doors don't have a great insulation value. Weather stripping was noted to be in reasonable shape at most locations. There are currenlty no rebates available from the GNWT for door replacements.



Savings: By upgrading your doors to new, ENERGY STAR rated, insulated doors I estimate that you will save approximately $350 per year in costs and reduce your energy consumption by 7.4 GJ



GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $125 per hinged door system is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.

</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

DOMESTIC HOT WATER



OIL TANK in a Diesel Electric Community



You currently have a 175 liter electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. As electricity is generated from diesel generators in your community, the overall efficiency is very low - diesel is used to produce electricity that is then used to produce your hot water. 



Savings: By replacing your electric hot water heater with an oil-fired semi on-demand water heater with an energy factor of 0.71, you could potentially save $ 890 per year in energy costs, but slightly increase your annual energy usage by 4.1 GJ. 



There is currently a $750 rebate available for installing an oil-fired water heater with an energy factor higher than 0.70 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</HotWaterSystem>
                <WaterConservation>

WATER CONSERVATION



Your house has an older 13 liter toilet that should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush!



Savings: By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



NOTE: You have not yet applied to Greenerr Homes so the information in this report is for information purposes only and not applicable unless you apply for and are approved for participation in the Greener Homes Program by Natural Resources Canada (NRCan)



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



Â In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

Â If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

Â Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

Â All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

Â It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

Â All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Inuvik, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Pass



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (your kitchen range hood does not vent outside, therefore the bathroom fan and clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 2.0 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and your domestic hot water tank vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting. Your wood stove could also experience back drafting when you open the door to add wood, during a burn.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

       

        - one bathroom fan

        - a clothes dryer

      

You may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan because they seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------





Fuel Costs Â Inuvik



The following December, 2021 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik:



Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.73 per kWh

Oil - $1.73 per liter 

Natural Gas - $37.21 per GJ delivered

Wood - $341.25 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $14.70 per bag, $682.50 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



